WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:06.003
300 units, recorded from 3 mice.

2
00:00:06.943 --> 00:00:08.103
How many samples is that?

3
00:00:09.033 --> 00:00:13.533
Units recorded from one mouse share that mouse: its genes and its drug dose.

4
00:00:14.583 --> 00:00:19.663
Units recorded in one session also share that day's alertness, and one probe track.

5
00:00:20.563 --> 00:00:24.563
If that mouse or session is unusual, its units tend to shift together.

6
00:00:25.617 --> 00:00:27.577
Take a small invented dataset.

7
00:00:28.497 --> 00:00:32.297
Two mice. Mouse A gave four units, and mouse B gave two.

8
00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:38.200
Pool every unit, and mouse A counts twice as much, only because it gave more units.

9
00:00:39.233 --> 00:00:42.793
Average within each mouse first, and each animal counts once.

10
00:00:43.693 --> 00:00:45.133
The animal mean comes out lower.

11
00:00:48.217 --> 00:00:51.477
For a claim about mice, the sample is 2, not 24.

12
00:00:54.650 --> 00:00:55.990
Now a harder test.

13
00:00:55.990 --> 00:00:59.050
Suppose a drug does nothing at all, on average.

14
00:01:00.083 --> 00:01:02.563
Record 100 units from each of 3 mice.

15
00:01:03.523 --> 00:01:05.163
Treat all 300 as independent.

16
00:01:06.283 --> 00:01:09.863
How often will a standard test call the drug effect significant?

17
00:01:16.017 --> 00:01:20.177
In a simulation with invented numbers, about 7 times in 10.

18
00:01:21.167 --> 00:01:26.647
The same test on the 3 mouse averages was significant only about 5 times in 100.

19
00:01:28.667 --> 00:01:33.047
That is the false-alarm rate the test is built to give, when its assumptions hold.

20
00:01:34.067 --> 00:01:38.107
Counting units as independent made the error bars far too narrow.

21
00:01:38.967 --> 00:01:40.507
This is called pseudoreplication.

22
00:01:41.567 --> 00:01:43.247
Are more units worthless, then?

23
00:01:44.167 --> 00:01:48.107
No. They average out the noise between units within each mouse.

24
00:01:49.117 --> 00:01:52.557
But no number of units averages out the differences between mice.

25
00:01:53.497 --> 00:01:55.077
The error stops shrinking at a floor.

26
00:01:56.117 --> 00:02:00.097
With the spread between mice held fixed, only more mice lower the floor.

27
00:02:02.217 --> 00:02:05.917
So report every level, and say which level your question is about.

28
00:02:06.917 --> 00:02:09.477
Each of these numbers is a step from your claim.

29
00:02:10.437 --> 00:02:12.217
Count your sample at the level of the claim.

30
00:02:13.217 --> 00:02:15.717
And none of this shows that a pathway is needed.

31
00:02:16.457 --> 00:02:17.597
That takes a manipulation.
